\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\ y & =2\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["81e3ecd1-48ab-5e50-ae5c-0b7642536209"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "1feddc4a-f369-516e-bbd5-46b6fc21adca" :answer/id #uuid "1feddc4a-f369-516e-bbd5-46b6fc21adca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "31144477-3b6f-549b-b737-e399729aa04b" :answer-field/id #uuid "31144477-3b6f-549b-b737-e399729aa04b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/choices ["1feddc4a-f369-516e-bbd5-46b6fc21adca"] :answer-field/correct "1feddc4a-f369-516e-bbd5-46b6fc21adca" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "f1ce0e10-a914-55d7-828c-95787df764e0" :answer/id #uuid "f1ce0e10-a914-55d7-828c-95787df764e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "16ee75b0-3d1d-53bc-bb02-8f1d4ed4a78a" :answer-field/id #uuid "16ee75b0-3d1d-53bc-bb02-8f1d4ed4a78a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/choices ["f1ce0e10-a914-55d7-828c-95787df764e0"] :answer-field/correct "f1ce0e10-a914-55d7-828c-95787df764e0" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "ac2fde5d-a37f-5387-93cb-e84570f83953" :question/id #uuid "ac2fde5d-a37f-5387-93cb-e84570f83953" :question/problem "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(3,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are\n\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordin